      "fallbackReason": null,
      "text": "⚡️🇹🇼 Taiwan’s military has tested how armored units would continue operating after a chemical attack during the fifth day of the annual Han Kuang exercise.\n\nThe scenario involved troops from the eastern Hualien-Taitung combat zone preparing to reinforce another region when infiltrators released a simulated chemical agent.\n\nContamination forced M60A3 tanks and CM21 armored personnel carriers to divert from their planned route and pass through a field decontamination station.\n\nPersonnel also practiced treating simulated casualties and chemical decontamination of soldiers.\n\nChemical warfare units established the decontamination site in about 30 minutes.\n\nEach vehicle passed through a multistage decontamination process, beginning with the detection and marking of contaminated areas.\n\nCrews then cleaned the chassis and tracks, washed the vehicle with hot water, applied neutralizing chemicals, rinsed the equipment, and conducted a final inspection.\n\nThe process took less than 10 minutes per vehicle and was intended to determine whether contaminated equipment could be safely returned to operations without contaminating personnel or other units.\n\n@medmannews",
